1. What is tokenization?
Tokenization is the process of splitting text into smaller units called tokens.
A token can be a word, part of a word, a character, or even punctuation, depending on the tokenizer.

INPUT TEXT
I love Python!

AFTER WORD TOKENIZATION
I | love  | Python  |  !
Each box represents one token in this illustrative example.

The computer can now process these individual units instead of treating the entire sentence as one unstructured string.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Types of tokenization

A. Word tokenization
Splits a sentence into words.
Example: I love AI → ["I", "love", "AI"]
Useful for understanding basic NLP and traditional text-processing techniques.

B. Sentence tokenization
Splits a paragraph into sentences.
Example: I love AI. I study NLP. → ["I love AI.", "I study NLP."]
Useful when processing paragraphs, articles and documents.

C. Subword tokenization
Splits words into smaller pieces when needed.
Example: unhappiness → un + happi + ness (illustrative only; actual splits depend on the tokenizer).
Very important for modern LLMs because their vocabularies can represent many words using reusable pieces.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Why do LLMs use tokenization?

Suppose you enter this prompt:
Explain artificial intelligence.

1. Input text
Explain artificial intelligence.
          |
2. Tokenization
Split the text into tokenizer-specific tokens
          |
3. Token IDs
Map each token to a numerical ID
         |
4. Embeddings
Convert token IDs into numerical vectors
         |
5. Transformer
Processes contextual relationships between tokens

Remember this important distinction:
- Token: A text unit.
- Token ID: An integer representing a token in the tokenizer's vocabulary.
- Embedding: A vector of numbers representing that token for the model.

