Tokenization is the process of dividing text into smaller units called tokens.
Q2. What is the difference between word and subword tokenization?
Word tokenization treats words as units, while subword tokenization can divide words into smaller pieces. Many LLM tokenizers use subword-based methods.
Q3. What is a token ID?
A token ID is the integer associated with a token in a tokenizer's vocabulary.
Q4. Are tokens and embeddings the same?
No. A token is a text unit, a token ID is its numerical identifier, and an embedding is a numerical vector.
Q5. Does one word always equal one token in an LLM?
No. A word may map to one token or several tokens, depending on the tokenizer. words with reusable pieces. The exact split depends on the tokenizer, and a word may sometimes remain a single token.
